# The voice host's entry in the recording launcher config
# (`cogs:robotcpu_record.textproto`), beside the production entry in
# `host_launch.textproto`.
#
# One message of simplelaunch's `AppConfig`, hand-written for the reason that
# one is: the renderer only knows about Clockwork processes and the host is not
# one. Paths are payload-relative, as in the production entry.
#
# The one difference is which speech configuration it names. A recording session
# transcribes everything said with no wake word, reads each transcript back over
# the speaker and bridges to nothing, which differs in kind from the site's
# arrangement rather than in a value: `[wake] policy = "bypass"`, `[brain] mode
# = "echo"`, no `[brenn]`. The payload has one speech-config slot per launcher
# entry and the entry names it literally, so a second arrangement is a second
# file rather than an override -- and it is the operator's own file for the same
# reason the site's is, because it names the STT and TTS services and the pod's
# link keys.
#
# The path below is where the build stages `REACHY_RECORD_SPEECH_CONFIG`; a
# payload built without one carries no file there, and `deploy-motion.sh
# --record` refuses it: a recording session whose transcripts are missing is a
# session that has to be run again.
#
# Merged into the recording config only. The production config and the harness
# twin name `host/speech.toml`, and no config names both files: a host started
# twice over one mic array is two claimants on the pod's audio link.
app {
  name: "voice_host"
  executable: "reachy_host"
  args: "--speech-config"
  args: "host/speech-record.toml"
}
